= Overview

== Some meta-level concerns

I do not want this to be a totalizing account of epistemology. It is, like all
good philosophies, about a _particular_ manner of engaging with the world. It is
itself a playful stance, an aesthetic, which can account for other aesthetics.
Hans' account fails to avoid totalization despite attempts.

== Key Concepts

=== Play

Play has several meanings. The one we focus on is transformative play, the play
of the metagame -- play which modifies the very rules of play. This sort of play
is entangled, in Noë's sense of the term. In addition to transformative play,
there is mechanical play (play broadly construed to include e.g. physical
notions of play), being-playful (play construed as a willingness to mold or
shift one's perspective, to _break_ the rules or to bend them), play as
rule-following (e.g. striving play, wherein one takes on the temporary goals of
a game so as to accomplish some meta-level goal).

Play as rule-following, our typical playful activity, is an interesting case
because it is simultaneously mechanical and meta-level. Our concerns are only
temporarily vested in the act of the game -- our larger goals live at a higher
level. Our goals in playing the game change the way in which the game is played.
For example if we are teaching a young person to play a game, we want to make
sure they maintain an interest by going easy on them, allowing them to win from
time to time. But if we are playing the game competitively, our brains kick into
high gear: what's wrapped up in the game is far more than an education, but
pride and identity, perhaps even prize money, a championship, one's livelihood.
So-called serious games also take on a more responsible role in our lives, for
example teaching nurses to cope with their jobs, or boot camp simulations
training soldiers for the battlefield. The history of games as a medium is tied
up with the development of war games in general, perhaps the most serious games
of them all.

How to consider ludic activity, i.e., messing around? Teasing, tussling, tossing
a ball around. As a kind of manipulative play, wherein the rules and boundaries
of our external world, be they social or physical, are tested?

-- Much of my analysis of play has to do with the _function_ of play more than
the phenomenology of it. There is not much discussion here of joy or desire.
That feels a bit out of scope -- I do not consider e.g. the evolutionary
psychology of play or its place in culture in large, but rather focus on its
role in our relationship to the Real (the unconscious, the noumenal, etc.), i.e.
its ontological and epistemological significance.

==== Taxonomy
Play happens when free movement occurs within a rigid structure.
Let's break that down. In what domains can play occur? What sort of structures give rise to play? What is 'free movement?'

These terms are overly broad. But perhaps their breadth is an asset. Free movement evokes the graceful swoops of a flycatcher, the stochastic movement of thermodynamic particles, and the fancy of an active imagination in equal measure. But these are vastly distinct domains: animal behavior, the movement of atoms, and theoretical postulation. In each case this behavior is restricted by bounds: the bird must fight against gravity and wind; the particles must respect the forces acting upon and against them; and the mind is bound by the rules of logic and conceptual framework in which it is situated. Without the wind and the pull of the earth there is no flight; without a wall to bounce against there is no heat pressure; without a constitutive framework there is no room for theorization, no concepts to bounce back against or deductive rules to follow. So we have established that *free movement cannot occur without restriction.* The phrase 'free movement within a rigid structure' is in fact redundant: there is no free movement without a rigid structure. But can we show the reverse? Does a rigid structure presuppose the possibility of free movement?

This one is more difficult. It seems quite clear that a universe solely consisting of static matter is possible, if not in fact more likely. But in this case, the _static_ nature gives away the game: if there is nothing for it to bound, is it a structure at all? Even the empty set provides a base structure: the _possibility_ to be filled. A monistic universe, consisting of only a single fact, perhaps of _nothing at all,_ is still structured by the rules of logical necessity, still conceptually bound by its negation. So it is the structure of our discourse that is limiting us. To solve this we need to take a step back and look at the structure which comprises our conceptual schemata.

Here I take inspiration from Zen Buddhist accounts of the structure of
consciousness. From whence do our conceptions arise in the first place? Nishida
calls this the place of absolute nothingness (_basho no zettai mu_). De Sassure
and Derrida might consider it the space in which the differential between
concepts occurs, the background on which _différance_ is made manifest. For
Heidegger it is similar to _das Nichts_ or _der Lichtung,_ the formless
nothingness from which all Being springs forth.

Each of these accounts point toward a meta-level consciousness, something that is beyond the direct layer of perception. I take Albahari's term _witness consciousness_ to describe this higher-level perception.

+ Structural rigidty
  - Mechanical play vs transformative play -- mechanical play occurs _within_
    the structure, is rule-bound; transformative play _changes_ the structure,
    occurs at a meta level
+ Domain
  - physical, mechanical, rule-bound
    - physical laws, rule-bound play, _deterministically automatable,_ "games" in the "game theory" sense.
    - This sense of play is not necessarily agentic. Agents definitely play like this, but not _only_ agents exhibit this sort of behavior.
      - Is this a credible notion of "play?" Is this not just 'stochastic
        behavior?' -- There seems to be a sort of _stategic incentive_ to reach an
        optimistic state. Physical phenomena certainly achieve this. Though it
        stretches the notion of play far beyond traditional credibility.
  - biological, chemical, transformative
    - agentic, theoretic, onto-epistemological -> a kind of transformative play
+ Levels
  - rule-bound play
  - metagaming
  - design


=== Trauma

As the unwilling counter to play. As the rigid structure which _prevents_ the
free movement. When the resistances become too much to bear. When one is trapped
within one's rigid ruleset and unable to escape it, despite the need to do so.
When symbolization cannot capture the real. (Lacan, Lecture XI)

A case study: An old woman in Kyiv grieves as her grandson is carried from the rubble of their bombed apartment building. How is she to cope? Her symbolic order has been utterly destroyed: the structures which upheld her life (presumably, revolving largely around her grandson) have been ripped from her. The encounter with the real cannot fit within the rules she has built for her life. Now, regardless of her will, she _must_ reconstitute her symbolic order. She has no choice. It is either resymbolize, or become delusional.

The process of healing for her will consist in finding the joy in the play
within her new symbolic order. To willingly accept the new order and participate
in the rule-bound structure which it offers. The resistances of the symbolic
order provide rigidity. They are adaptive. But they can be deceiving. It
behooves us to keep our strictures in mind, and to maintain a willingness to
play within their boundaries, to push up against them and see where they give.
Much like learning carpentry, you must first strip a few screws before you know
when to stop.

=== Ecstasy
- Being outside of oneself
- Key argument: This is the default. Self-awareness is not the default point of view.

Self-awareness occurs precisely at the moment when our perceptual rules break
down, when our symbolizing structures need to be updated.

We are by default 'lost in the sauce' of our symbolic structures. We are too
busy playing the game to think existentially, ontologically. We are alerted to
the ontic when we have an encounter with the aesthetic, that is, with beauty; or
when we are confronted with the traumatic.

=== Entanglement

Hans talks about grafts on fields of production. This is basically the same
thing as the entanglement. Our fields of production came from random sources and
are based on a tradition, and it is the acknowledgement of the tradition which
makes the new grafts possible. It is both transcendent and immanent, meta and
base level, in a circle, or better, a Möbius strip. Cf the hermeneutic circle.

Production is the production of _anything,_ production generally construed.
Production of goods, of services, of action in general. Play then as the way
production happens. Cf Hans ch 2, Production. Play is how production happens, on
'fields of play' (or aesthetic fields), which consist of a series of grafts
stratified into a culture or tradition. Acknowledgement of the tradition lends
itself to a modification of the rules (metagaming) which produces new grafts and
potentially opens us up to new fields of play.

=== Desire

Desiring-production at the heart of Hans' conception of play. Two kinds of production: ecstatic production and violent production. Both are intertwined.
This is my conception of play and trauma; willing play and unwilling play;
willing reorientation and unwilling reorientation; it is the difference between
exhilaration and chaos.

=== The Aesthetic

The aethetic: ways of _seeing;_ they are 'fields of play' or sets of rules to follow, they are ontologies and epistemologies which shape our worldviews. This is _felt_ as well as it is theorized. It is _experienced,_ _lived_ in the full sense of the term. It is a manner of our Being-in-the-world, the fundamental root of our existential conditions. But what early Heidegger misunderstood is that there are many of them, and that we are always switching between them; that the fields of play are intermeshed, and the boundaries can blur. (How does this square with the rule-bound conception? Ethics as aesthetics, or the rules by which an aesthetic is bound? -- but that's a different essay.)

=== Design and Metagaming

Design is the art of crafting experiences. Metagaming is the practice of
grafting. Both tie into the aesthetic, into fields of play: they create new
rules, bring fields together or drive them apart. Design is the art of changing
an individual's understanding, construed as a way of relating to the world
(one's Being). Metagaming is the process by which design happens. The
philosopher's goal, as the artist's, is to be a designer in this sense: to
create ways of seeing, to bring new ways of Being into the world; to graft
together fields of play and create new kinds of production, new desires. The
task of the artist is to design an experience; the task of the philosopher is to
train the designer. (Is that the right phrasing?)

